*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***
`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's
nine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A
main-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER
HAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were
written during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.

SEVERITY RAISED MED -> HIGH, on a fact DERIVED rather than assumed.  The row said the fix
was *presumably* the same as [[sgirandompixel]]'s and that the observable differed.  **The reachability is now
established and it is the SAME documented-mode structure, not merely the same shape.**

Measured at HEAD, three commands:
  * the PUT colour switch implements exactly FOUR cases -- `DE_MODE_TYPE_' CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR -- and its
    `default:' is `buf[0..3] = random()';
  * `crmfbreg.h' documents **FIVE** colour types; the fifth is `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB  0x000000f0';
  * `grep -c YCRCB src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **0**.  It is handled nowhere.

And it is reached by an ordinary register write: `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK', mask `0xf0', so a
guest selecting YCRCB lands on the random arm directly.

*** SO THIS IS `#504's OWN SENTENCE AT A SECOND SITE: four of five DOCUMENTED modes work and the fifth
returns random data. ***  `#504` was graded HIGH on exactly that warrant, and this arm is arguably worse in
one respect -- `sgirandompixel' put a random value in `*color' for the CALLER to store, whereas this writes
four random bytes into the tile through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)' with no intermediary.

**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and the severity should be read with it: NO WITNESS.**  Nothing here shows a guest
driver selecting YCRCB -- only that the model accepts it and answers with random data.  The rung-3 attempt
looks cheap for the same reason `#504's did (`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this
code path; the colour type is a different field of the same mode word), and it is OWED before this ships.

**The GET side does NOT share the defect** -- its `default:' reads the raw 32-bit value rather than inventing
one, so this is a PUT-only arm.  That asymmetry is itself worth carrying into the design: the two default
arms of one function already disagree about what an unhandled colour type means.

Note this is a DIFFERENT defect from [[sgirebufuninit]] at the same lines: that one is about bytes NOBODY
assigns; this is about bytes assigned from `random()'.  A fix for either leaves the other standing.

*** READY TO RUN AS THE NEXT ROUND.  Three things established 2026-09-07 while `#505` worked in the
same function, none of which this row had. ***

**1. IT IS A DIFFERENT SWITCH FROM THE ONE `#504` FIXED, and the row should say so plainly** -- I nearly
recorded this as the `#499` symmetric-cell shape and it is not.  `#504` de-fanged the **addressing-mode**
default (`default: fatal("unimplemented dst_mode %i ..."); *color = random();`).  This row is the
**colour-mode** default of a different `switch` in the same function:

    default:buf[0] = random();  buf[1] = random();
            buf[2] = random();  buf[3] = random();
            // TODO.
            fatal("[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\n", color_mode);
    }
    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS|PHYSICAL);

**There is no `break` and no decline: control falls straight into the write**, so four bytes of host
`random()` land in guest memory at a guest-chosen tile offset.  Same family as `#504`, same function,
different arm -- *related by idiom, not by the one-cell-repair pattern.*

**2. FULLY GUEST-REACHABLE, established by reading rather than assumed.**  `color_mode = mode &
DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`, and `mode` is the caller's `src_mode`/`dst_mode`, read straight out of `de_reg[]`
from `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC`/`CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` with **no filtering anywhere on the path**.  The four
implemented types are 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30; `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` is **0xf0 and is documented in
`crmfbreg.h`** but unimplemented, so a guest selecting a real, named mode reaches this arm.

**3. A WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT AWAY.**  `#505` built `regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`,
a rung-3 probe that drives an XFER copy on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32` with both ladder controls and a
sibling accept row, and whose `setup()` now takes **both** src and dst modes.  Pointing its `dst_mode` at
`LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_32 | 0xf0` puts YCRCB into the PUT switch and reaches this arm.

  *** THE ORACLE IS THE HARD PART AND IT NEEDS SAYING BEFORE THE ROUND STARTS: THE DEFECT'S OUTPUT IS
  RANDOM, SO NO VALUE ROW CAN PIN IT. ***  `#504` hit exactly this and its answer is the precedent --
  assert that the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value, i.e. that the write was DECLINED, rather
  than trying to assert anything about what was written.  A determinism oracle is refuted in advance by
  this project's own record: two seats showed on `#504` that stack reuse can make a wrong value
  deterministic, so *"the same value twice"* proves nothing.

**FIX SHAPE: the option set has a member already chosen for its sibling.**  `#504` used DECLINE + LATCHED
ANNOUNCE with its own bit (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10`), for the reason `#498` measured -- the four
handlers share one struct, so a shared latch is silenced by the other site's ordinary traffic.  **This arm
needs its OWN bit, `1 << 11`**, and `#504`'s comment already says the enum ends there on purpose so the
next maintainer finds it.  *That is a transferred in-tree warrant, not a "cannot hurt" addition.*

*** PASS-1 AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG AND THE
DISPOSITION SURVIVED. ***

**VERDICT:** *"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE is justified, with an unconditional return from the PUT
default.  But the brief misstates #504's oracle and the write's byte count; depth 8 also exposes
uninitialized buffer bytes."*

*** CORRECTION 1 -- THE ORACLE.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE COST A ROUND. ***  My brief said
`#504`'s precedent asserts *the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value*.  It does not.  The seat
opened the probe: `lin_b is fg` expects **`FG_VAL = 0x77665544`**, not the pre-paint `0x11111111`,
because a declined source GET **preserves the caller's foreground value**, which the following PUT
then writes.  In the seat's words, *"the probe explicitly rejects preserving the destination as a
different disposition."*

  **So `#504` warrants the latch-and-return IDIOM and does NOT supply the unchanged-destination
  ORACLE I attributed to it.**  Those are different transfers and I ran them together.

  **THE ORACLE THE SEAT PROPOSES INSTEAD, and it is stronger than either:** observe **ZERO
  `MEM_WRITE` CALLS** from an unsupported PUT, *alongside* unchanged destination bytes -- because
  that pair **distinguishes "no write" from "same final value"**, which neither assertion does
  alone.  It also notes that if removing the RNG use is contractual, that must be observed
  SEPARATELY.

  *And a sentinel value will not rescue a value oracle here:* each assignment converts a SEPARATE
  `random()` result to `uint8_t`, so the whole-word improbability argument `#504`'s old witness used
  does not establish an impossible byte pattern.

*** CORRECTION 2 -- THE BYTE COUNT, and it is the SAME WIDTH-AXIS LESSON `#505` PAID FOR HOURS
EARLIER. ***  My brief said *four* bytes of `random()` are written.  The write is `bufdepth` bytes,
not four: at bufdepth 8 the arm fills `buf[0..3]` with RNG and ships **four further UNINITIALISED
stack bytes** with them; at bufdepth 1 or 2 fewer than four RNG bytes land.  **"Four" is true only
at bufdepth 4** -- the width every example happens to use.  *That is the exact shape of `#505`'s
clause-(d) floor breach, arriving in the next round's brief the same day, written by the person who
had just repaired it.*  Overlaps [[sgirebufuninit]]; the round must say which defect owns which
bytes.

**OPTION SET: CONFIRMED COMPLETE, and the grep found three in-tree alternatives worth naming:**
  * the helper's own GET default already provides a raw ABGR-shaped fallback (`dev_sgi_re.c:358`);
  * **PCC2 has a real ACCEPT precedent** (`dev_pcc2.c:427`) -- a read served from backing storage
    before the default diagnostic;
  * **SGI's ROP diagnostics latch BY VALUE** (`dev_sgi_re.c:850`) rather than one bit per site.

None establishes correct YCRCB PUT semantics.  *"Mirroring the GET fallback would repeat the
unsupported-authority problem you warned about.  The YCRCB name establishes an encoding; it does not
establish packing, conversion, or depth semantics."*  IMPLEMENT is defensible in principle but no
permitted source supplies an implementation or a demonstrated driver selection.  **No additional
warranted disposition outside the option set** -- which is the option-set rule returning a clean
negative for once, and that is worth recording as such.

**THE LATCH BIT IS CONFIRMED AVAILABLE AND THE OBJECTION TO ONE BIT IS RECORDED:** occupied bits
enumerate as 0..10, `warned` is `uint32_t`, so **`1 << 11` is free**.  The strongest objection is
diagnostic coverage -- *an earlier unnamed mode suppresses a later YCRCB announcement* -- and
per-mode latching has in-tree precedent (the ROP site above).  One bit stays consistent with this
helper's existing policy, so it is a judgement the round must make explicitly rather than inherit.

**FIX SHAPE (Q2), in the seat's words:** *a terminal default arm containing a conditional
announcement followed by an **unconditional function return***.  Note this is the SKETCH RULE's
territory -- the PUT branch's write sits after the switch and is shared by all five arms, so the
return must be checked against the whole enclosing function before it is written.

*** AND A WARNING THAT SAVES THE ROUND A FALSE START: `#504`'s PROBE CANNOT BE REUSED UNCHANGED. ***
*"#504's `lin_b is fg` remains green under edits confined to this colour default.  Both of that
probe's mode constants use RGBA, so it never selects the PUT colour default.  Its setup cannot be
transferred unchanged."*  `#505`'s probe is the better base -- its `setup()` already takes both src
and dst modes -- but the same check applies to it and must be run, not assumed.

**ENUMERATION CONFIRMED, with a qualification I did not have:** the twelve default selections are
eleven unnamed (`0x40`..`0xe0`) plus the named `0xf0` YCRCB, and they reach the arm *"when the
preceding coordinate/addressing/tile-validity checks pass"*.

*** THE ORACLE QUESTION, ANSWERED BEFORE THE ROUND STARTED -- AND THE NAIVE IMPLEMENTATION WOULD
HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN.  codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`.
***

**VERDICT:** *"Your distinction is conditionally sound.  A correctly scoped write-attempt observer
works for Round A; **the existing stepped probe supplies none.**  This fork's debugger watchpoints
offer a qualified alternative."*

**MY DISTINCTION SURVIVES, with a precision I had not stated.**  A count separates *no request* from
*a request carrying the existing bytes* -- but *"zero calls does not establish correct rejection
unless the unsupported arm was ACTUALLY REACHED: an earlier return or an operation that never ran
also produces zero."*  That is the attribution problem, and it means the round owes a reach control
beside the count.

*** THE MECHANISM EXISTS: debugger WRITE WATCHPOINTS, checked in the common memory path BEFORE
device/cache/RAM dispatch, and neither PHYSICAL nor NO_EXCEPTIONS excludes this helper's write.  A
pty driver can observe them with NO instrumented build. ***  (`memory_rw.c:273`.)

*** AND IT HAS TWO DECISIVE LIMITS, THE FIRST OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN: ***
  1. **The check requires `!single_step`, and the existing probe's `run_ops()` uses `step`.**  So
     *"merely adding watchpoints and counting their messages to that execution scheme could report
     ZERO DESPITE WRITES."*  The row would have reported "no write happened" while writes happened.
     **A watchpoint oracle therefore requires the FREE-RUNNING probe shape, not the stepped one.**
  2. It also requires `!about_to_enter_single_step` and **SETS that flag after its first report**, so
     later pixel writes inside the same drawing command go unreported.  What Python would count is
     *printed watchpoint reports*, not `MEM_WRITE` calls -- watchpoints carry no hit count.

**SO THE ACHIEVABLE CLAIM IS BOUNDED, and the round must say so rather than overclaim:** the
mechanism supports an **any-attempt-to-this-range** witness when observation is active throughout the
relevant execution.  It does NOT support *"exactly N calls"* or *"no writes anywhere from this
helper"*.  The conclusion must read **"no observed write attempt to this range under these validated
conditions"** -- watching the destination cannot exclude a write to an UNINTENDED address, which is
exactly the failure mode [[sgirebufuninit]] and the `#505` tiled row both hit in other forms.

**THE SHIPPABLE DESIGN, in the seat's own construction:** verified arm reach and completed execution,
an active observer that DOES detect a supported write to the same range (the liveness half), then no
matching write report for the rejected operation, with unchanged verified destination bytes as
corroboration and the announce count testing "announce once" separately.  **That is four rows, not
one**, and knowing it now is worth more than discovering it after the fix.

*This is the carrier's `!single_step` family arriving through a NEW mechanism.  The carrier records
that a stepped probe cannot observe INTERRUPT DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`'s
"do not cause interrupts while single-stepping").  The WATCHPOINT case is the same guard on a
different feature and was not written down.*

*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_design_20260907.txt`).
VERDICT: REVISE -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE **with a `return` before the PUT transfer**.  It
refuted TWO of the brief's premises, and one of them was the brief's central design claim. ***

**S3 WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE PREMISE THE WHOLE 'COPY THE SIBLING' READING RESTED ON.**  I wrote that
`sgirandompixel` fixed *the GET side of this same helper*.  It did not: **it repaired the
ADDRESSING-MODE default at `:235`, which sits BEFORE GET and PUT diverge and returns for both
directions.  The COLOUR GET default at `:358` still performs a raw conversion.**  Two different
arms -- so the sibling is a precedent for the DISPOSITION and not for the site, and its comment
cannot simply be copied.

**S5 WAS WRONG, AND THIS ONE UNBLOCKS THE ROUND.**  I claimed the decline's 'what does `buf` hold'
question was the SAME question as [[sgirebufuninit]]'s, making that row a prerequisite.  codex:
*"A declined PUT need not leave anything defined in `buf`, provided nothing consumes it.  The helper
itself owns the memory write.  Its callers receive no buffer to transfer afterward."*

    remove the fill, fall through   -> transfers indeterminate stack bytes   REJECT
    zero the span, fall through     -> overwrites destination with zero      needs a warrant
    RETURN from the PUT default     -> preserves destination bytes           PREFERRED

> **So the two rows are INDEPENDENT and there is no patch ordering.**  This round decides whether an
> unsupported PUT writes anything at all; `sgirebufuninit` decides how paths that DO transfer avoid
> consuming uninitialised bytes.  *Initialising the buffer would neither remove the four `random()`
> calls nor justify writing zeros for an unsupported format.*

**S1 needs a width qualification:** four bytes are ASSIGNED randomly, but the transfer length is 1, 2,
4 or 8.  A narrow transfer exposes only the prefix; an eight-byte transfer additionally consumes four
uninitialised bytes.

**S2's inference was a CORRELATE and the real population is twelve, not one.**  `grep -c YCRCB` = 0
proves only that one NAMED constant is absent.  Enumerating `mode & 0xf0`: sixteen values, **four
explicit PUT cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR) and TWELVE reaching `default:`** -- eleven unnamed plus YCRCB.
The DE register handler stores the mode without filtering the type nibble, and both line and rectangle
callers pass it through unchanged.  *The named-constant search was the same error that cost the
`asicbadaddr` census a 4x undercount.*

**FAULT IS AVAILABLE HERE and my brief implied otherwise** -- `memory_rw.c:603` delivers MIPS
exceptions.  codex rejects it anyway on better grounds than availability: the helper returns `void`,
its transfers request `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and the outer device handler returns success, so FAULT would
need an error-propagation design and a warrant for the guest-visible exception.

**THE LATCH MUST BE AN INDEPENDENT BIT, NOT `SGIRE_W_BADMODE`** -- sharing it would let an ADDRESSING
failure suppress the COLOUR diagnostic.  That is `#490`'s measured latch-collision class, predicted
here before the code exists.

**Q4, the predicted escape:** condition the new `return` on `bufdepth == 4` -- the canonical clause-(d)
shape, conditioning the repair on a field the transfer consumes.  **codex expects `rgb tiled64` to stay
green under it**, because that row varies the GET SOURCE's addressing and width while keeping a linear
four-byte RGBA destination: *"its name does not establish PUT coverage."*  The proposed closure is the
destination axes together -- twelve unsupported types x four widths x four address modes = **192
combinations** -- with whole-span checks including eight-byte spans.  *Source-based prediction; no
mutant built.*

**Q3, the witness:** source inspection supports a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on stock IP32 and codex
found no machine-description blocker.  But **the sibling probe's green rows are NOT a witness for this
row**: its setup fixes the DESTINATION to RGBA, so varying its source-mode argument never exercises the
colour PUT default.  Record **'source-reachable; rung-3 attempt pending'** -- codex ran nothing, as
instructed, so the attempted-probe prerequisite is NOT yet discharged.

*** A CONSEQUENCE OF THE DECLINE DESIGN, found by the codex seat on the SIBLING brief
(`sgimtedepth4dead`) and cross-filed here because it is this row's design that it bears on. ***

*"A helper return before accessing the buffer leaves the caller's colour unchanged.  MTE still
performs the dependent PUT, and its `bg` persists across iterations -- so a declined GET can reuse an
earlier source value."*

**That is distinct from buffer initialisation and it bites the agreed repair.**  The design settled
for this row is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with a `return` before the transfer -- and on the MTE
path the caller's `bg` is a loop-scope variable, so a declined pixel does not leave the destination
untouched: **it writes the PREVIOUS pixel's colour.**  A decline that silently smears the last good
value across a run of unsupported pixels is a different wrong answer from `random()`, and arguably a
more plausible-looking one.

*The round owes an answer: does the decline return before the PUT as well, and if so what does the
destination hold?  This was not visible from this row's own brief, which framed the question around
`buf` rather than around the caller's colour variable.*

*** DESIGN PASS 2, 2026-09-07: I CONFLATED A DECLINED GET WITH A DECLINED PUT.  PASS 1's DESIGN
STANDS UNCHANGED. ***

The PUT-default `return` exits **before** `memory_rw(... MEM_WRITE ...)`, so whatever the caller's
`color` holds, that declined PUT does not transfer it.  Leaving the destination's existing bytes
intact is the honest disposition for an unsupported write.  **The `bg` observation is about the
dependent-PUT problem, which is a different row.**

**AND MY S4 DID NOT ESTABLISH REACHABILITY:** MTE builds modes from addressing and depth bits only,
leaving the colour type at CI, and its one colour override sits behind the unreachable `depth == 4`.
**Neither construction reaches this PUT colour default**, so the MTE `bg` behaviour does not bear on
this row at all.

*Two further qualifications on my wording: MTE reloads `bg` from `CRIME_MTE_BG` on **each transfer
invocation**, so a declined GET retains the latest value from THAT invocation or the register's
initial value -- never a previous command's local `bg`.  And "the whole rectangle deterministically
receives the last good colour" is too strong independently, because CI's accepted PUT initialises
only `buf[0]` and wider depths consume uninitialised bytes.*

**THE LATCH GRANULARITY IS CONFIRMED AS APPROPRIATE AND ITS LIMIT STATED:** one message per device
instance for the latch's lifetime -- so a thousand declined pixels produce one line, **and none if an
earlier command already set the bit** (the struct is zeroed at init; there is no per-command reset).
It deliberately cannot distinguish one declined pixel from a rectangle; reporting extent would need
counting or a command summary.

**RESIDUAL FOUND: bounding the PUT warning does not bound every warning from the operation.**  The
**GET colour default still logs without a latch**, and DE can perform that GET for a
destination-dependent raster operation before ever reaching the declined PUT.  So a round that
latches only the PUT arm leaves a guest-drivable unlatched diagnostic in the same code path.

**THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT:** change the new `return;` to `break;`, keeping the latch and the
announcement.  Execution resumes at the memory write and consumes the uninitialised buffer -- **it
breaks the no-write contract without restoring `random()`**.  `rgb samemode` and `rgb tiled64` both
expected to stay green (supported RGB/RGBA, never reaching the default).  *The coverage gap is wider
than MTE: the scoped scripts never select an unsupported destination colour type at all, so their
green results cannot establish this decline.*

*** THE WITNESS PROBLEM IS CHARACTERISED, AND IT IS HARDER THAN THE ROW ASSUMED.  From the codex
ORACLE answer (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`), read in full
2026-09-07 -- most of this had been sitting unread. ***

**TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS FIRST, and the second changes the FIX, not just the witness:**

1. *"The buffer lacks a declaration initializer; it is not NEVER INITIALIZED.  PUT arms assign
   prefixes, and GET fills the requested transfer length."*  The row's shorthand was sloppy.
2. *** "THE MISSING `break` IS NOT WHAT PERMITS THE WRITE.  The write is AFTER the switch, so adding
   `break` would still reach it.  **The proposed return matters.**" ***  A `break`-shaped repair
   would look right and do nothing -- exactly the sketch-versus-enclosing-control-flow rule this
   project minted after four readers proposed a `return` into a function with a common tail.

*** THE OBSERVER EXISTS, AND IT IS DISQUALIFIED BY THE PROBE'S OWN EXECUTION MODE. ***

The fork has debugger WRITE-WATCHPOINTS, and the common memory path checks them **before**
device/cache/RAM dispatch -- *"neither `PHYSICAL` nor `NO_EXCEPTIONS` excludes this helper's
write"* (`memory_rw.c:273`).  A matching attempt prints `WATCHPOINT: write`, which a pty driver can
count with no instrumented build.  **But two conditions kill it as written:**

    memory_rw.c:273   the check requires  !single_step
                      -- and sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's run_ops() uses `step'.  Adding
                      watchpoints to that scheme "could report ZERO DESPITE WRITES."

    same check        also requires  !about_to_enter_single_step, and SETS that flag after its
                      first report -- so subsequent pixel writes inside the SAME drawing command
                      go unreported.

> **This is the carrier's single-stepping rule arriving on a new mechanism.**  It already records
> that a stepped probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`: *"Do not cause
> interrupts while single-stepping"*).  **Watchpoints are a second capability gated the same way**,
> and the same discriminator applies: ask what the observation depends on before designing around
> it.

**WHAT THE MECHANISM CAN AND CANNOT SUPPORT, in codex's words:** it supports an
**any-attempt-to-this-range** witness *"when observation is active throughout the relevant
execution"* -- i.e. FREE-RUNNING, not stepped -- and even then only the first report survives the
flag.  It *"cannot automatically support 'exactly N calls', or 'no writes anywhere from this
helper'.  A literal, attributed call count needs a suitable host-side observer or `harness:`
witness."*

*** AND Q3 DEMOLISHES THE CHEAP ORACLE: "unchanged destination bytes prove only unchanged OBSERVED
CONTENTS." ***  Equality at the observation points is also consistent with a same-value write, a
write followed by restoration, a write to somewhere else, no drawing operation at all, or an earlier
helper return.  **Two of those are live here rather than theoretical**: the helper returns on an
invalid tile pointer (`dev_sgi_re.c:274`), and *"the memory path can discard an out-of-RAM write
while returning success; corresponding reads can return zeros"* (`memory_rw.c:662`).

> **So a destination-equality row needs a control establishing that the pre-paint REALLY EXISTS and
> that a SUPPORTED operation through the same path actually lands** -- otherwise "unchanged" is
> indistinguishable from "nothing ever happened", which is the vacuity shape in its purest form.

**THE TWO VIABLE PATHS, neither cheap:**
* **(a) a FREE-RUNNING probe with a write-watchpoint** -- any-attempt semantics, first report only,
  and it must abandon the stepped `run_ops()` idiom the sibling probes use;
* **(b) a `harness:` witness with a host-side observer** -- an attributed call count, which the
  ladder admits only when the round's subject IS the apparatus, so it would need care not to become
  the rung-1 shortcut the ladder exists to refuse.

**STATUS: this row is NOT held for fable5 and is the only HIGH row that is not** -- but its blocker
is now a characterised capability gap rather than an open question, and neither path is a session's
work.  *Recorded in full because most of this analysis had been sitting unread in the artifact since
it was produced, which is this session's most-repeated failure.*

*** DESIGN PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  FIVE SEATS, UNANIMOUS ON BOTH QUESTIONS -- and the strongest answer
REFUSED TO CERTIFY ITSELF, which is the part worth keeping. ***

**THE DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, implemented as candidate (b) -- skip the
PUT's `memory_rw` for a declined mode.  Never an early `return`.**

*** grok's REFRAMING IS BETTER THAN MY OPTION LIST AND IT DISSOLVES THE QUESTION THE ROW WAS FILED
ON. ***  The row asked *"what should a declined BUFFER FILL leave behind?"*, treating `buf` as the
analogue of `#504`'s `*color`.  It is not:

> *"Destination of a PUT is guest memory at `tileptr + ofs`, not `buf`.  `#504` declined the
> helper's OUTPUT.  The PUT output is the store.  Skipping the store leaves whatever the guest
> already had -- deterministic, no invented colour, no host bytes.  `buf` can stay uninitialised
> because it is not sent."*

So the fix is not a fill policy at all.  **The question "what should the buffer contain" was the
wrong question**, and my option (a) (zero-fill) was demoted to a fallback for exactly one case:
if some tail consumes `buf` or assumes the store happened.

**AND IT CORRECTED MY (a) IN PASSING:** zero-filling only `buf[0..3]` to mirror the `random()` shape
is NOT (a) -- `bufdepth` can be 8, so that form *"is this arm already instantiating
`sgirebufuninit`"*.  If (a) is ever taken it must define the whole payload.

*** IT DECLINED TO CERTIFY (b), NAMING EXACTLY THE GAP: *** *"That tail is not in the packet.  I
will not guess `x++`, a dirty bit, a return value, or a use of `buf`.  I cannot certify (b) against
'nine lines down.'"*  That is this project's own sketch rule, applied by a seat to its own
recommendation, unprompted beyond the caution in the brief.

## *** BOTH OF ITS UNCHECKABLE PREMISES ARE NOW SETTLED BY ME, BY OPENING THE FILE ***

**1. THERE IS NO COMMON TAIL.**  `horrible_getputpixel()` is `void`, and after the PUT store the
`else` runs the GET path and the function ENDS -- `}` closing the else, `}` closing the function,
nothing between them.  No dirty bit, no counter, no return value, no later use of `buf`.
**So (b) is safe**, and the `put_ok` flag form grok gave is belt-and-braces rather than necessary --
which is still the right way to write it, because it cannot become the early-`return` trap under a
later edit.

**2. `fatal()` DOES NOT EXIT.**  `src/core/debugmsg.c`: `va_start; va_debug; va_end;` and return --
no `exit()`, no early-out.  grok flagged that if `fatal()` aborted, *"the random fill is dead and
the guest-visible row collapses."*  It does not abort, so **the random bytes really do reach
`memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)` and the defect stands.**

*That premise should have been IN the packet.  The carrier records `fatal()`'s non-exit as a
standing fact and I did not inline it -- the settled-premises rule exists precisely so a packet-fed
seat need not park on something already known.*

## ROUND SCOPE: TWO ROUNDS, 5 of 5, each with its own reason

Not one round with `sgirebufuninit`, despite the two sitting in the same `switch`.  **minimax gave
the sharpest form:** *"a regression in the CI/depth-8 path and a regression in the YCRCB path
produce the same test failure"* -- i.e. one oracle cannot tell them apart, which is exactly the
round-scope rule's test rather than a preference.  grok: *"They sit in the same `switch`.  That is
not the test."*  **And it named the trap explicitly: zero-initialising `buf` at its declaration
would silently convert this round's (b) into (a) and smuggle `sgirebufuninit`'s fix in with it.**

*This was asked as an open question arguing both ways, so the unanimity is worth something -- unlike
unanimity on a brief-supplied FACT, which this project has measured to be worth nothing.*

## RESIDUALS RAISED, NOT YET DISPOSED

* grok: the arm *"is already confused -- it announces unimplemented (`TODO` + `fatal`) and then
  IMPLEMENTS with noise.  The announce is the right instinct (unlatched, per-pixel, therefore wrong
  shape).  The fill is the defect."*  So the existing `fatal()` becomes the latched announce; it is
  not added, it is fixed.
* **12 of 16 mode values reach `default:` and only `0xf0` is documented.**  grok accepted the
  arithmetic (`16 - 4`, needing no header) but declined the reachability claim as *"claims about
  other code, not in the packet"* -- correctly.  **A round taking this owes the reachability
  check**, and `IMPLEMENT` (member 7) for YCRCB is a separate design: *"no YCbCr packing,
  subsample, or byte order in this packet; 11 of the 12 values are not even named."*
* Q4/Q5 (detector and witness) are in the seat answers and NOT yet consolidated here -- that is an
  UNREAD-REVIEW debt on this row until it is.

*** THE Q4/Q5 UNREAD-REVIEW DEBT IS DISCHARGED, AND IT WAS WORTH PAYING: THE PANEL'S OWN MAJORITY
DETECTOR WOULD HAVE SHIPPED AN ESCAPABLE ROW. ***

I recorded the detector and witness answers as unread.  Reading all five:

## THE DETECTOR -- convergent, and it is a DESTINATION CANARY, not "looks non-random"

Pre-fill the target bytes with a distinctive non-zero canary, execute ONE PUT with a declined mode,
read back, and **pass iff the canary is bit-identical** -- i.e. the oracle is *the store did not
happen*, not *the value is not random*.  A liveness control in the SAME script repeats with a
defined mode (RGBA or CI) and the canary MUST change; without it a dead PUT path greens the round.

*** THE HOLE, and three of the five seats were in it: DO NOT PIN THE TEST TO `0xf0` ALONE. ***
deepseek and kimi both proposed YCRCB `0xf0` as *the* test mode, kimi arguing it is the value a real
guest would plausibly select.  grok:

> *"A mutant `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random would escape.  Use at
> least one UNDOCUMENTED value (`0x40` is the first hole) as the primary; `0xf0` can be a second
> vector, not the only one."*

**That is a real escape against the majority design.**  The documented mode is the most tempting
choice and the weakest one, because a mutant can special-case exactly it.

**FOUR MORE STRENGTHENINGS, one per seat, none of them duplicated:**
* **grok** -- vary `bufdepth` over at least {1, 4, 8}, so a mutant that skips the store only when
  `bufdepth == 4` dies.
* **minimax** -- make the oracle **CONJUNCTIVE**: the latch transitioned 0 -> 1 *during that pixel*
  (record the PRE-state) AND `memory_rw` was not called for it.  Either conjunct alone is escapable,
  and recording the pre-state defeats a mutant that pre-sets the latch at init.
* **kimi** -- assert the announce count is **exactly 1**, which is what actually pins the latch.
* **glm** -- assert the ADJACENT bytes are unchanged too, since the write is only `bufdepth` bytes
  at `ofs`; and run twice asserting identical transcripts.

**AND grok'S HONEST LIMIT ON ITS OWN ORACLE:** keeping `buf[i] = random()` while setting
`put_ok = 0` leaves the canary intact, so a destination-only oracle cannot see the leftover
`random()` calls.  It calls that *"a successful (b), not an escape, PROVIDED the patch also deletes
the four `random()` calls -- same edit, not a second oracle"*, and explicitly declines to make
"`random()` is not called" the oracle because an LCG or `rand()` defeats it.

## *** THE BLOCKING PREMISE THREE SEATS RAISED INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW SETTLED -- BY ME, IN TWO READS,
## AND IT SETTLES IN FAVOUR OF THE ROUND. ***

glm, kimi and deepseek each named the SAME single fact as the one that decides whether a witness
exists at all: **does the mode-register write path mask or reject `0xf0` (and the eleven
undocumented values) BEFORE they reach this helper?**  glm put the consequence sharply: if it does,
*"no witness exists, the round is documented-only, and '12 of 16' then describes host-reachable
states no real guest can reach."*

**THERE IS NO VALIDATION.**  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`:

    :1098  /*  Treat all registers as read/write, by default. ... */
    :1112  d->de_reg[regnr] = idata;              <- unconditional, unmasked

and the `switch (relative_addr)` below it reaches `case CRIME_DE_MODE_DST:` only to `debug()` the
value and `break` -- no mask, no rejection, no range test.  `draw_primitive()` then reads
`d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000)/4]` straight back, and `horrible_getputpixel()` computes
`color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`.

> **So a guest store of ANY 32-bit value to CRIME_DE_MODE_DST is kept verbatim, and bits 4-7 select
> among all 16 colour modes -- twelve of which reach the `random()` arm.  The witness is
> constructible and the round is NOT documented-only.**

*The file's own comment is the authority here, which is the strongest form: it is not that I failed
to find a validator, it is that the code states it treats these registers as raw read/write.*

**THE REMAINING UNSETTLED ITEM is smaller than it looked:** kimi asked whether `#504`'s witness
itself selected a declined mode.  That is a question about a shipped probe, answerable by reading
`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py`, and it is no longer load-bearing now that the register path
is settled.

*** THE CENSUS IN THIS ROW'S TITLE IS RIGHT ABOUT THE NAMED CASE AND UNDERSTATES THE SET BY TWELVE
TIMES.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here before recording. ***

The title says `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB -- a DOCUMENTED mode -- reaches it'.  True, and it is ONE of
TWELVE values that do.

**MEASURED, not taken from the seat:**

    crmfbreg.h:328-333   CI 0x00, RGB 0x10, RGBA 0x20, ABGR 0x30, YCRCB 0xf0, MASK 0xf0
    dev_sgi_re.c:210     uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;      *** NO SHIFT ***

    domain of (mode & 0xf0):  16 values -- 0x00 0x10 ... 0xf0
    handled:                   4        -- 0x00 0x10 0x20 0x30
    UNHANDLED:                12        -- 0x40 0x50 0x60 0x70 0x80 0x90 0xa0 0xb0 0xc0 0xd0 0xe0 0xf0
                                            = ELEVEN UNNAMED ENCODINGS + the named YCRCB

**THE MISSING SHIFT IS THE WHOLE POINT.**  The constants look like a small enumeration -- 0, 1, 2, 3
scaled by 16 -- and reading them that way suggests four handled plus one named gap.  But the code
never normalises the field, so the switch compares raw masked values and every one of the sixteen
the mask can produce is a distinct case label.  *A derivation that assumes the obvious shift reads
exactly like a correct one, which is why this was enumerated rather than argued.*

> **THIS IS THE `asicbadaddr` SHAPE EXACTLY, and the carrier already records why it matters: a
> detector built from the wrong census walks the named value, covers a twelfth of the defect, and
> reports full coverage.**  A round that ships a row for YCRCB alone would do precisely that.

*** TWO REACHABILITY REFINEMENTS FROM THE SAME ANSWER, which the row did not have: ***

* **The producer does not filter.**  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` stores incoming register words without
  narrowing the colour-format bits, and `draw_primitive()` passes the stored mode to the pixel
  helper unnarrowed.  So the drawing-engine caller genuinely exposes all sixteen encodings.
* **The MTE caller constructs only CI or RGBA**, so NEITHER default is reachable through it.  That
  is a real distinction: the round's reachability argument belongs to the drawing engine only, and
  saying "the device reaches it" without naming the caller would be too strong.

*** AND A SECOND, SEPARATE OBSERVATION ABOUT THE BUFFER, recorded because it is NOT the same
defect and must not be folded in silently: `buf` is EIGHT bytes, not four. ***
`dev_sgi_re.c:307` is `uint8_t buf[8];` with the `#100` comment *"bufdepth can be 8 = 1<<((mode>>8)&3)"*.
The PUT `default:` assigns only `buf[0..3]` from `random()`.  **What the remaining four bytes hold,
and whether any bufdepth reads them, is NOT established here and is not claimed.**  It is written
down as an open question for the round rather than as a finding.

*** SEAT AND PROVENANCE, because it bears on how this row gets worked. ***  codex refused this row's
WITNESS brief twice -- the construction-shaped ask ("give the exact guest instruction sequence") is
content-filtered, see [[codexcyberflag]].  The identical subject in ANALYSIS form ("how many cases,
what is the domain, what does the default do") answered in full with a verdict line.  **The seat was
never unavailable; the ask was wrong**, and two firings were spent before that was measured.

